did you see labyrinth as a kid have you  rewatched it lately you might pick up on  some deep themes subtle references and  creepy overtones if you do almost every  adult will recognize the voice singing  the song over the opening credits of  labyrinth as that of pop culture icon  david bowie even before he appears in  character as the goblin king even today  bowie remains one of the most famous  performers in rock history although  unless they're music buffs younger kids  are unlikely to know much about him  while he may have been a highly  influential figure in music in the 20th  century kids without an interest in rock  and pop probably aren't all that aware  of his body of work of course labyrinth  serves as a fantastic introduction to  bowie as both actor and musician his  performance as the goblin king continues  to be one of his most beloved and it's  no wonder given that the character  exemplifies the rockstar glam bowie was  so familiar with  throughout his career bowie played with  persona and identity and although he was  clearly playing a specific role in  labyrinth he extended that tradition to  the film the end result is an infinitely  fascinating and instantly iconic villain  one whose true appeal only adults will  be able to fully grasp just fear me  love me do as i say and i will be your  slave  jennifer connolly landed the lead role  of sarah in labyrinth after an  exhaustive search that saw all sorts of  big names audition  in the end connolly beat out a bevy of  future stars including jane krakowski  yasmine bleath sarah jessica parker mary  stuart masterson lara dern and lily  taylor at the time connolly was only 14  years old and new to the entertainment  business but labyrinth wasn't her first  film she had already starred in three  other movies although none of them  cemented a legacy as strong as jim  henson's fantasy tale  of course adults will know that conley  went on to become a star and respected  actor one who eventually won an oscar  for best supporting actress for her work  in 2001's a beautiful mind today  connolly tends to take roles in mature  projects that kids aren't too likely to  watch so they may not realize just what  an impressive career sarah went on to  enjoy the funny thing is of course that  connolly looks remarkably similar today  to the way she did in 1986 making her a  highly recognizable star even for kids  that only know her from this 35 year old  film  there are some huge names behind the  scenes of labyrinth from george lucas to  jim henson but one that many youngsters  may not recognize is that of terry jones  who is credited with writing the film's  script terry jones was one of the  members of the groundbreaking comedy  troupe monty python and while the  group's name may ring a bell with some  kids they're unlikely to be able to put  any faces to the name jones who died in  2020 never had quite as high a public  profile as other monty python members  however he was pivotal in shaping their  unique brand of comedy and even directed  or co-directed many of their movies  including the iconic monty python and  the holy grail at the same time jones  became a noted children's book author  which is likely why henson wanted him to  tackle the screenplay for labyrinth the  irony however is that although jones is  the only writer credited with penning  the movie there were over 25 versions of  the script written between 1983 and  1985. many of them weren't by jones  either in fact it was a draft with  changes by famed screenwriter elaine may  that was used during production in the  end due to all the differences from his  original screenplay jones believed that  the finished product of labyrinth didn't  feel very much like his story  sarah is a pretty relatable character  for kids who know they've gotta grow up  but who aren't sure they want to take on  the responsibilities of adulthood  however grown-ups are more likely to  feel that her petulance and lack of  consideration for others are pretty  insufferable as is this little  catchphrase oh it's not fair  it's not fair it's not fair  you say that so often  i wonder what your basis for comparison  is  sure she has some heroic qualities  including intelligence grit and  determination but her immaturity makes  it hard to really like her especially  early on in the film it's possible that  if sarah was younger it would be easier  to forgive her youthful peevishness but  at 16 sarah seems a bit old to feel so  put upon after all this is a character  who gets mad at her baby brother for  taking her teddy bear and whose room is  full of toys and children's books it's  not that adults would necessarily find  sarah more likeable if she acted like a  grown-up but she might be less  frustrating if she actually took on the  responsibilities of being a teenager but  hey in a sense sarah learning to do so  is kind of what the whole movie is about  the mythology behind labyrinth is  clear-cut when someone wishes the  goblins will take someone else away they  happily grant their wish removing the  unwanted individual and making them a  goblin too but what's not as clear is  why jareth as the goblin king wants to  add to his already vast legion of  goblins while he may be their king  jareth seems to be human and perpetually  annoyed by the goblins he rules so why  would he want to turn toby into one  despite this jareth does everything in  his power to make sure he can keep baby  toby first by offering sarah everything  she wants in exchange for letting toby  go and then when she rejects his offer  by trying to thwart sarah's rescue  attempts  while kids probably won't question  jareth's motivations many adults will  recognize something doesn't quite add up  here  of course jareth may be bound to uphold  the tradition of turning unwanted people  into goblins to expand his kingdom but  since the movie never mentions this it  ultimately leaves you wondering just why  he needs another goblin in the first  place  kids could be forgiven for thinking that  the costumes and hair of the human  characters in labyrinth are in keeping  with the bizarre fantasy land in which  the story is set adults however will  recognize the film style as being  emblematic of an even stranger time the  1980s the 80s had a distinct look and  sound and the movie fully commits to it  consequently the costumes involve a lot  of vests flowy shirts puffy sleeves  sparkles and color while sarah's  costumes are relatively understated for  the time the goblin kings are  consistently over the top something  that's especially obvious in his hair  and makeup with an elaborate eye shadow  and gravity-defying hair bowie's look in  the film is both iconic and a perfect  encapsulation of the stylistic excess of  the decade the movie's 80s aesthetic  extends to its music too including the  score by trevor jones and the songs  which were written and performed by  bowie featuring a whole lot of electric  guitar and synthesizer the music may  seem dated to younger ears but bowie's  songs are undeniably ear worms  magic dance in particular is a silly yet  hugely memorable part of the movie one  that will have even modern day kids  dancing along  from the movie's opening moments sarah's  strong attachment to fairy tales and  fantasy is obvious  and while kids may miss it the play  sarah is rehearsing at the beginning of  the film is called the labyrinth this  would indicate that the plot of the  movie is sarah's fantasy come to life  and formed by the play she spent her  time memorizing yet the film slips so  seamlessly from the real world of  babysitting and responsibilities to that  of goblins and mazes that the land of  labyrinth comes across more like a real  place than a fantasy of course that may  be by design for someone who's as  imaginative as sarah the line between  fantasy and reality might not be all  that strong nonetheless the lack of  clarity leaves a lot for adults to  interpret that kids are likely to  overlook including how each of the  characters exemplify sarah's various  fears and desires and how the story  itself is a metaphor for learning to  accept your more adult responsibilities  at one point as sarah makes her way  through the labyrinth she falls down a  hole the scene is reminiscent of alice  falling down the rabbit hole at the  beginning of alice in wonderland but in  this case the hole is lined with living  stony hands that catch sarah before she  hits the bottom the hundreds of hands  lining the hole and their penchant for  coming together to form faces and talk  is undeniably compelling in fact the  sequence is generally a remarkable feat  of creativity one of the many moments  that make labyrinths so captivating for  both kids and adults at the same time  though it's hard not to look at all  those hands gripping the teenage sarah  and think about the matter of consent  given that those hands appear to be  conscious and not particularly worried  about where they're grabbing her adults  may find the sequence a little more  disturbing than kids  there's a lot to love about labyrinth  but the relationship between the goblin  king and sarah shades into unsettling at  times essayist alison stein noted in the  atlantic that from the movie's outset  jareth clearly doesn't just want toby as  she wrote he wants sarah he wants her to  love him and his longing increases  becoming more and more creepily clear as  the movie progresses there's both a  paternal appeal and stranger danger in  jareth as an adult this is more than a  little disconcerting and no sequence in  the movie is more troublesome than the  scene in which sarah imagines attending  a masquerade ball  over a song about falling in love the  masked goblin king makes a beeline for  sarah and as they dance the charge  between them doesn't feel at all  appropriate of course for kids and even  teenagers the goblin king's menacing  magnetism may make him compelling even  attractive adults though will be more  likely to recognize the disturbing  undertones this evokes and see jareth  for the genuinely creepy villain that he  is  when sarah finally makes it to the  goblin king's castle she finds herself  trying to get to both toby and jareth in  a room right out of relativity one of mc  escher's most famous works the black and  white lithograph depicts a room full of  staircases but only some seem to obey  gravity while others go up or to the  side meanwhile the people walking along  the staircases and standing on the  landings all seemed to conform to  different gravitational pulls the idea  escher so vividly brought to life in his  art is envisioned in live action in the  scene in labyrinth this is something  adults and possibly even some kids will  notice instantly although younger  children who aren't familiar with the  art world won't understand the reference  still there are clues in the movie that  this is a deliberate homage not only  does a print of relativity hang on the  wall of sarah's room the closing credits  of the film include a special entry  thanking the estate of m.c escher sarah  may not be the easiest character to like  but she learned some valuable lessons  during her adventures in the labyrinth  these are lessons that kids may  appreciate too even if they won't be  nearly as aware of them as adults will  much of what sarah learns centers on the  realities of life when an adult isn't  there to save you for example sarah  frequently complains that what she  experiences isn't fair but when hoggle  makes a similar complaint sarah realizes  that he's right sometimes life isn't  fair and that's just the way it is it's  not fair no it isn't  but that's the way it is  after that she stops complaining about  fairness and faces the challenges of the  labyrinth head-on fair or not by the end  of the film sarah is able to recognize  that even though her journey has left  her more mature and capable she still  needs the friends she made along the way  who are able to bring enjoyment and  whimsy into her life it's a smart but  subtle lesson about the need to balance  the responsibilities of adulthood with  freewheeling fun every now and then  something even the most serious adult  needs to remember  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

